---
import { candidateVersion } from '../lib/product-plans.mjs';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { appVersion, issuesUrl, sourceUrl } from '../lib/release.mjs';

const description = 'ZackEyes safety model for local hooks, quiet bridge failures, optional candidate SSH observation and private Usage archives.';
const securityStructuredData = {
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  '@id': 'https://zackeyes.vercel.app/security#model',
  headline: 'ZackEyes security and safety model',
  description,
  about: [
    'local-first macOS app',
    'Claude Code hooks',
    'Codex CLI hooks',
    'Unix socket bridge',
    'quiet failure mode'
  ]
};
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Security - ZackEyes"
  description={description}
  canonical="https://zackeyes.vercel.app/security"
  structuredData={[securityStructuredData]}
>
  <div class="grain" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="site-shell">
    <SiteHeader />

    <main id="top">
      <section class="policy-hero compact-page-hero" aria-labelledby="security-title">
        <div class="policy-shell">
          <div class="section-eyebrow">Local safety model</div>
          <h1 id="security-title">Security and safety model.</h1>
          <p class="policy-lede">
            ZackEyes is local-first by design. It connects Claude Code and Codex CLI events to the macOS notch through identifiable local hooks and a Unix socket bridge, while preserving the terminal workflow if the app is not running.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="policy-band" aria-labelledby="boundaries-title">
        <div class="policy-shell policy-columns">
          <div>
            <h2 class="section-title" id="boundaries-title">Write boundaries.</h2>
            <p class="section-copy">
              ZackEyes-managed hook entries are intended to be visible, reversible, and scoped to the integration files they need.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="policy-list">
            <article>
              <h3>Backups before hook writes</h3>
              <p>Every write to `~/.claude/settings.json` and `~/.codex/hooks.json` is preceded by a timestamped backup so users can inspect or restore prior settings.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>Small configuration surface</h3>
              <p>Claude integration changes are limited to hook and status line entries. Codex integration uses `~/.codex/hooks.json` and does not need the CLI config file.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>Explicit non-goal</h3>
              <p>ZackEyes should never read or write `~/.codex/config.toml`. Hook installation should abort instead of guessing if a JSON file cannot be parsed safely.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>Identifiable entries</h3>
              <p>ZackEyes-managed commands contain the literal string `zackeyes`, which makes them searchable in local hook files during audit or uninstall.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>Source-code auditable</h3>
              <p>Every boundary above is enforced in code that lives in the open under the MIT license. Read the hook installer, the bridge, and the socket protocol yourself at <a href={sourceUrl} rel="noopener">github.com/yangshiqi/ZackEyes</a> — no closed binary doing magic behind the scenes.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="faq-band" aria-labelledby="runtime-title">
        <div class="section-inner">
          <div class="section-eyebrow">Runtime behavior</div>
          <h2 class="section-title" id="runtime-title">Bridge and failure mode.</h2>
          <div class="spec-grid">
            <article>
              <span>Bridge</span>
              <strong>Unix socket</strong>
              <p>Agent hook events are normalized locally and sent to the macOS app over a Unix socket. The bridge is for local status, not a hosted dashboard.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <span>Failure</span>
              <strong>fail quietly</strong>
              <p>If ZackEyes is closed or unavailable, controlled failures write nothing to stdout, exit 0, and return control to Claude Code or Codex CLI.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <span>Audit</span>
              <strong>User removable</strong>
              <p>Use Settings → Integrations → Uninstall Integrations… before quitting and removing the app. Deleting ~/.zackeyes also erases saved Usage history, SSH host configuration and any existing journal files; copy data you want to keep first.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="policy-band" aria-labelledby="candidate-boundaries-title">
        <div class="policy-shell">
          <h2 class="section-title" id="candidate-boundaries-title">Candidate SSH and archive boundaries.</h2>
          <p class="section-copy">These boundaries apply to the unreleased v{candidateVersion} candidate. The published download version remains v{appVersion}.</p>
          <div class="policy-list">
            <article><h3>SSH observation</h3><p>The system SSH client uses public-key authentication and strict host-key checking. ZackEyes does not accept unknown hosts automatically, show password prompts, or change SSH configuration. Remote metadata includes project paths, agent/model, tool names, state and last activity; prompts, replies and tool arguments are excluded. Remote cards cannot approve requests or jump to a local terminal.</p></article>
            <article><h3>Private Usage archives</h3><p>Local counts and resumable import state use a private SQLite database under ~/.zackeyes/usage/ with directory mode 0700 and file/sidecar mode 0600. Unsafe permissions, symlinks and unsupported storage formats are rejected. Source transcripts and the previous saved ledger are not modified; removed source history cannot be recreated without saved counts.</p></article>
            <article><h3>Question answering and Demo</h3><p>Supported Claude question answers require explicit opt-in and fall back to the terminal after two minutes. Demo approvals and answers target synthetic sessions only. Remote requests stay in the remote terminal.</p></article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="policy-band" aria-labelledby="report-title">
        <div class="policy-shell docs-layout">
          <div>
            <h2 class="section-title" id="report-title">Report a concern.</h2>
            <p class="section-copy">
              Use the public issue tracker for install problems, hook behavior, compatibility reports, security concerns, and requests for clearer controls.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="policy-list">
            <article>
              <h3>What to include</h3>
              <p>Share your ZackEyes version, macOS version, Mac model, agent CLI, the observed hook file state, and reproduction steps.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>Public feedback channel</h3>
              <p><a href={issuesUrl}>GitHub Issues</a> is the feedback channel for security model questions, bugs, feature requests, and workflow concerns.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <SiteFooter />
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
